---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Korean Class', url: '/community-classes/korean/' },
  { name: 'Free Korean Class Johannesburg', url: '/free-korean-class-johannesburg/' },
];

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Course',
  name: 'Free Korean Class in Johannesburg',
  description: 'A free Korean language class for beginners in Johannesburg, taught every Sunday afternoon in Parkmore, Sandton.',
  provider: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Johannesburg Bible Study Church',
    url: 'https://joburgchurch.co.za',
  },
  educationalLevel: 'Beginner',
  inLanguage: ['en', 'ko'],
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    price: '0',
    priceCurrency: 'ZAR',
  },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Free Korean Class in Johannesburg | Sunday Afternoon Beginner Group"
  description="Looking for a free Korean class in Johannesburg? We teach beginners every Sunday 2:00pm in Parkmore, Sandton. Learn Hangul, conversation, and culture in a welcoming group. No textbook. Since 2019."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-max max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-sm text-blue-600 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Korean Class Johannesburg</p>
      <h1 class="heading-1 mb-6">Free Korean Class in Johannesburg: The Honest Guide for Total Beginners</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-8">
        You searched <em>free korean class johannesburg</em>. Maybe you saw a K-drama and got hooked. Maybe your partner is Korean. Maybe Duolingo took you as far as Duolingo can. Whatever the reason, you are now considering a real, in-person class. Here is everything you need to know before you book a seat in Parkmore.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-purple-50 to-pink-50 border border-purple-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-10">
        <p class="font-semibold text-purple-900 mb-2">Who this page is for:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-purple-900 text-sm">
          <li>✓ Johannesburg residents interested in Korean language or culture</li>
          <li>✓ Complete beginners (no Hangul knowledge required)</li>
          <li>✓ People tired of Duolingo and wanting real conversation practice</li>
          <li>✓ Anyone who wants a low-stakes, friendly environment to learn</li>
          <li>✓ People preparing for travel, study, or work in Korea</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Why Korean? Why Johannesburg? Why Us?</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        Korean has become one of the most popular languages in Johannesburg over the past five years. Korean food, music (K-pop), film (K-drama), and skincare have all driven curiosity. Add in growing business and academic links between South Africa and Korea, and you have a strong motivation to learn.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        Most language schools in Johannesburg charge R3,000-R8,000 for an 8-week beginner Korean course. Our class has been free since 2019 — taught by Ms. Dora, a former Korean language teacher who moved to Johannesburg. It is funded by voluntary donations from the church community and a small materials budget from Johannesburg Bible Study Church.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">What You Will Learn</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        The 16-week beginner track covers:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 text-body">
        <li><strong>Weeks 1-4:</strong> Hangul alphabet (consonants, vowels, combinations), pronunciation, basic greetings</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 5-8:</strong> Self-introduction, numbers, time, asking for directions</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 9-12:</strong> Restaurant Korean, ordering food, basic grammar (particles, verb endings)</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 13-16:</strong> Daily routines, family, weather, present tense conversation</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        By week 16, you can have a 5-minute conversation with a native Korean speaker about your life, their life, the weather, and where to find the nearest Korean restaurant.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Why We Teach Korean in a Church Building</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        The honest answer: we have the space, and Sunday afternoons were empty. The deeper answer: learning a language is a doorway into understanding a different culture. That kind of openness is at the heart of what a Bible study community does — we are curious about the world, and we make space for that curiosity.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        You will not be preached at. You will not be asked to convert. You will not be pressured to attend Bible study. The Korean class is its own thing, run with the same care and welcome that we bring to our Wednesday evenings.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">What to Expect on a Typical Sunday</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        Here is a snapshot of what 2:00pm looks like in Parkmore:
      </p>
      <ol class="space-y-3 mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 text-body">
        <li><strong>2:00pm — Settle in.</strong> Coffee is on. Most people are already at their seats chatting in English by 1:50. We start at 2:00 sharp.</li>
        <li><strong>2:05pm — Casual announcements.</strong> Any birthdays, any visitors, any Korean cultural events coming up (we sometimes watch a movie together at 4pm after the class).</li>
        <li><strong>2:10pm — Review.</strong> Quick go-round of last week's phrases. Anyone with questions from the homework (which is optional).</li>
        <li><strong>2:20pm — New material.</strong> The teacher introduces new vocabulary, a grammar concept, or a cultural topic.</li>
        <li><strong>2:50pm — Pair practice.</strong> You pair up (or stay solo) and practice the day's conversation with a worksheet.</li>
        <li><strong>3:15pm — Group conversation.</strong> Whole class tries the dialogue together. Mistakes are celebrated.</li>
        <li><strong>3:40pm — Wrap up and what's next.</strong> Homework (optional), next Sunday's topic, goodbyes.</li>
        <li><strong>3:50pm — Optional hangout.</strong> Coffee. Most people stay another 15-30 minutes just chatting. New friendships form here.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Practical Details</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 text-body">
        <li><strong>When:</strong> Every Sunday, 2:00pm to 4:00pm</li>
        <li><strong>Where:</strong> Parkmore community room, 11th Street, Sandton 2196 (exact address on confirmation)</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> Free since 2019</li>
        <li><strong>Materials:</strong> We provide everything — worksheets, writing practice sheets, audio. Bring a notebook if you want.</li>
        <li><strong>Parking:</strong> Free street parking on 11th Street and surrounds. Sundays are quiet.</li>
        <li><strong>Public transport:</strong> Gautrain Sandton station is a 10-minute Uber away.</li>
        <li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Wheelchair accessible. Restroom facilities available.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Who Comes to the Class</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        A typical Sunday afternoon has 8-15 students. The mix changes over time, but recent demographics:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 text-body">
        <li><strong>By age:</strong> Mostly 22-35. A few 18-22 year-olds from Wits and UJ. A small number of 40-65 year-olds.</li>
        <li><strong>By motivation:</strong> About 40% preparing for travel or exchange. 30% K-drama/K-pop fans. 20% mixed-heritage couples (Korean and other). 10% academic/business.</li>
        <li><strong>By nationality:</strong> Mostly South African, plus Korean, Chinese, Nigerian, American, and European students.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Why "Free" Matters</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        Money should not be a barrier to language learning. We have had students who are unemployed, students, single parents, and recent immigrants. The class is supported by voluntary donations from the church community. We never ask for money in the class itself, and we never ask students to donate.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        If you can afford to support the class — buy us a coffee, donate R50, or sponsor our photocopying budget — we are grateful. If you cannot, come anyway. The door is open either way.
      </p>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">Alternatives We Are Not</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        We want to be honest about what this class is and is not:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 text-body">
        <li>✗ <strong>Not accredited.</strong> You will not get a certificate at the end. If you need one for a job or university, take a course at Wits Language School or University of Pretoria.</li>
        <li>✗ <strong>Not a cram school.</strong> We move at the pace of the slowest in the room. If you are an advanced learner, ask about the intermediate group.</li>
        <li>✗ <strong>Not a sales funnel.</strong> We are not trying to sell you Korean products or convert you. We are just teaching Korean.</li>
        <li>✓ <strong>Free, friendly, beginner-focused, since 2019.</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">FAQ</h2>

      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Do I have to be Korean to attend?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">No. About 5% of our students are Korean heritage learners; the other 95% have no Korean background. Mixed-heritage couples are very welcome.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Is the class really free? What is the catch?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">Yes, really free. No catch. We have run it for seven years on voluntary church support and donations from regular students.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Can I attend if I work in central Johannesburg?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">Yes. The class is in Parkmore (near Sandton), which is closer to Joburg Central than most people think — usually 15-25 minutes by car.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">What is the difference between this and a Korean language school?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">We have 8-15 students per class; schools have 20-40. We are free; schools charge R3,000-R8,000. We move slower and build community. Schools are more rigorous and structured.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Will I be able to read Korean after one Sunday?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">Yes. Hangul is designed to be learnable in a day. By the end of the first 90 minutes, you will read all 14 consonants and 10 vowels and begin combining them.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-3">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Should I learn Korean before going to Korea?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">Yes. Even basic Korean transforms the trip. Order food in Korean, read signs, greet people — it all matters. Most tourists who try a few phrases get overwhelmingly warm responses.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="card mb-10">
        <summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">Can I attend if I am not Christian?</summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700">Of course. The class is a language class. You will not be asked to do anything outside the language learning.</p>
      </details>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">What Real Students Say</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 mb-10">
        <blockquote class="card bg-blue-50 border-blue-100">
          <p class="italic text-gray-700 mb-2">"I came for three months to test the class, expecting to leave. Six months later I am still here and I can hold a basic conversation with my partner's Korean family."</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">— Lerato M., Sandton</p>
        </blockquote>
        <blockquote class="card bg-purple-50 border-purple-100">
          <p class="italic text-gray-700 mb-2">"Ms. Dora is patient, knowledgeable, and actually makes Hangul click. I tried Duolingo for six months. After two Sundays here, I had learned more than in those six months."</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">— David O., Randburg</p>
        </blockquote>
        <blockquote class="card bg-amber-50 border-amber-100">
          <p class="italic text-gray-700 mb-2">"Honestly, the best part was the community. I moved to Joburg from Cape Town knowing zero people. Now I have a friend group of people who also enjoy Korean food and language."</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">— Sarah K., Fourways</p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>

      <h2 class="heading-2 mb-4 mt-10">What Happens After the Beginner Track</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">
        Students who complete the 16-week beginner track can join the intermediate group, which meets on a separate schedule. Topics at intermediate level include:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6 list-disc pl-6 text-body">
        <li>Past tense and storytelling</li>
        <li>Korean TV shows watched with discussion</li>
        <li>Cooking classes (we cook together, in Korean)</li>
        <li>Reading short Korean articles</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-purple-600 to-pink-600 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 text-center mt-12">
        <h3 class="heading-2 text-white mb-3">Reserve Your Seat for This Sunday</h3>
        <p class="text-purple-100 mb-6 max-w-xl mx-auto">2:00pm. Parkmore, Sandton. Bring nothing but curiosity.</p>
